    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d582-a1">

                <pb facs="0945" n="809" sameAs="frus:frus1888p1/pg809"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Fairchild to Mr.
                            Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Treasury Department</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-05-28"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 28,
                        1888</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your letter of the 25th instant, inclosing copy of a note to your Department
                    from the British minister, dated the 21st <pb facs="0946" n="810" xml:id="pg810"
                    /> instant, inquiring whether the immigration of certain Indians from British
                    Columbia into Alaska is contrary to the immigration laws of the United
                    States.</p>
                <p>Section 1 of the “act to regulate immigration,” approved August 3, 1882, applies
                    only to such passengers “who shall come by steam or sail vessel from a foreign
                    port to any port within the United States.” And section 22 of the so-called
                    “shipping act,” approved June 26, 1884, in effect abolishes the capitation tax
                    on immigrants from contiguous foreign territory.</p>
                <p>It would therefore appear that the immigration referred to was not a violation of
                    the immigrant laws of the United States.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">C. S. Fairchild</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
